<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 112 0 R
/T (Modern processors contain multiple CPU cores. The operating-system scheduler decides where runnable processes and threads execute and when they are allowed to run. Good scheduling aims to keep cores usefully occupied while preserving cache locality. Poor placement can leave some cores underused, overload others, or move work between cores and reduce the benefit of cached data.)
/E (Modern processors contain multiple CPU cores. The operating-system scheduler decides where runnable processes and threads execute and when they are allowed to run. Good scheduling aims to keep cores usefully occupied while preserving cache locality. Poor placement can leave some cores underused, overload others, or move work between cores and reduce the benefit of cached data.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 112 0 R
/T (This investigation studies two related scheduling choices: processor affinity and workload distribution. Processor affinity restricts the CPU cores on which a worker is allowed to execute. Workload distribution determines how tasks are assigned to workers. The experiment compares unrestricted Linux scheduling, workers pinned to separate cores, and workers pinned to one core. It also compares static block distribution, static round-robin distribution, and dynamic distribution through a shared queue.)
/E (This investigation studies two related scheduling choices: processor affinity and workload distribution. Processor affinity restricts the CPU cores on which a worker is allowed to execute. Workload distribution determines how tasks are assigned to workers. The experiment compares unrestricted Linux scheduling, workers pinned to separate cores, and workers pinned to one core. It also compares static block distribution, static round-robin distribution, and dynamic distribution through a shared queue.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 112 0 R
/T (A Python multiprocessing prototype is used so that CPU-bound work can run in separate processes rather than being limited by the Python Global Interpreter Lock. The Linux affinity interface is accessed through os.sched_setaffinity\(\). An optional Rust extension can be used for the computation kernel, provided the same kernel is used for every compared configuration.)
/E (A Python multiprocessing prototype is used so that CPU-bound work can run in separate processes rather than being limited by the Python Global Interpreter Lock. The Linux affinity interface is accessed through os.sched_setaffinity\(\). An optional Rust extension can be used for the computation kernel, provided the same kernel is used for every compared configuration.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
