360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 113 0 R
/T (Processor affinity is a restriction or preference that controls the CPU set on which a process or thread may run. On Linux, taskset and the sched_setaffinity system call can be used to inspect or set affinity. In this experiment, the modes are: none \(leave placement to the default scheduler\), spread \(pin each worker to a different permitted CPU\), and single \(pin every worker to one permitted CPU\).)
/E (Processor affinity is a restriction or preference that controls the CPU set on which a process or thread may run. On Linux, taskset and the sched_setaffinity system call can be used to inspect or set affinity. In this experiment, the modes are: none \(leave placement to the default scheduler\), spread \(pin each worker to a different permitted CPU\), and single \(pin every worker to one permitted CPU\).)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 113 0 R
/T (Affinity may help preserve cache locality and reduce unnecessary migration. However, strict pinning can prevent the scheduler from moving a worker away from a busy core. Therefore, pinning is not assumed to be faster in every case.)
/E (Affinity may help preserve cache locality and reduce unnecessary migration. However, strict pinning can prevent the scheduler from moving a worker away from a busy core. Therefore, pinning is not assumed to be faster in every case.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
