1382 0 obj
<<
/Title (This investigation studies two related scheduling choices: processor affinity and workload distribution. Processor affinity restricts the CPU cores on which a worker is allowed to execute. Workload distribution determines how tasks are assigned to workers. The experiment compares unrestricted Linux scheduling, workers pinned to separate cores, and workers pinned to one core. It also compares static block distribution, static round-robin distribution, and dynamic distribution through a shared queue.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1339 0 R
/Prev 1361 0 R
/Next 1403 0 R
>>
endobj
1383 0 obj
<<
/Title (The planned measurements are wall-clock execution time, throughput, speedup and load imbalance. The investigation is designed to show whether pinning improves locality, whether a distribution method balances unequal task costs, and whether adding workers continues to improve performance.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1339 0 R
/Prev 1403 0 R
/Next 1362 0 R
>>
endobj
