1467 0 obj
<<
/Title (Processor affinity is a restriction or preference that controls the CPU set on which a process or thread may run. On Linux, taskset and the sched_setaffinity system call can be used to inspect or set affinity. In this experiment, the modes are: none \(leave placement to the default scheduler\), spread \(pin each worker to a different permitted CPU\), and single \(pin every worker to one permitted CPU\).)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1455 0 R
/Next 1468 0 R
>>
endobj
1468 0 obj
<<
/Title (Affinity may help preserve cache locality and reduce unnecessary migration. However, strict pinning can prevent the scheduler from moving a worker away from a busy core. Therefore, pinning is not assumed to be faster in every case.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1455 0 R
/Prev 1467 0 R
>>
endobj
1469 0 obj
<<
/Title (A core's caches can retain data recently used by a worker. If the worker later executes on another core, some of that cached data may not be available in the same way, increasing memory-access cost. CPU migration is one possible reason for performance changes, but this prototype does not directly count cache misses or migrations unless optional Linux performance counters are collected.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1456 0 R
>>
endobj
1470 0 obj
<<
/Title (Main contribution)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1446 0 R
/Prev 1458 0 R
/Next 1459 0 R
>>
endobj
1471 0 obj
<<
/Title (Together, these studies indicate that multi-core performance depends on both where work executes and how work is divided. Affinity can preserve locality and reduce migration, while strict pinning may restrict load balancing. Dynamic work distribution can improve utilization when task costs differ, but coordination overhead can offset the benefit, particularly for small tasks.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1460 0 R
>>
endobj
